<html>
<title>Program Configuration</title>
<body>


<h1>Program Configuration</h1>

<p>
For every computer a default program configuration is defined. For most computers this is the first available 1, 2 or 4K of RAM. For the Studio clones it is the cart area and for the Cidelsa the game ROM.
</p>

<p>
A program configuration must be defined to save debug or type information, i.e., the information defining what locations are 1802 code, data and possibly pseudo-code. The same goes for using the <strong>Insert</strong>, <strong>Delete</strong> and <strong>Search</strong> features; see the <a href="DirectInsert.htm">Insert &amp; Delete Code</a> and <a href="DirectSearch.htm">Search</a> sections.
</p>


<h2>Basic configuration</h2>

<p>
The program configuration interface is shown below:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_conf.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Dir</strong> button</li>
<li>(2) <strong>Program name</strong> text field</li>
<li>(3) <strong>Code start</strong> text field</li>
<li>(4) <strong>Code end</strong> text field</li>
<li>(5) <strong>(Program) end</strong> text field, default equal to Code end</li>
<li>(6) <strong>S/P</strong> text field, used on COMX for <strong>S</strong>lot code and on ELF for the EMS <strong>P</strong>age</li>
<li>(7) <strong>Load</strong> button</li>
<li>(8) <strong>Save</strong> button</li>
</ul>

<p>
The example shown above defines a code area of hex 4400-53FF and a data area between hex 5400-5FFF. After this configuration is defined, pressing the <strong>Save</strong> button (8) will store a binary file <tt>mycode.bin</tt> from memory locations hex 4400-5FFF as well as save type information in a file <tt>mycode.debug</tt>.
</p>

<p>
When <strong>Save</strong> is pressed, a configuration file name is also requested; this file will save all configuration data such as program name, directory, code start, etc.
</p>

<p>
To load an existing configuration file, use the <strong>Load</strong> button (7).
</p>

<p>
Keep all data either at the start of the code area, or after it in the data area between <strong>Code end</strong> and <strong>(Program) end</strong>. Data can also be used mixed in with code as long as the <tt>LVL</tt> macro is used to load the data address (see the <a href="DirectAssembler.htm">LDL, LDRL, LDV and RLDL instructions</a> section).
</p>

<p>
If no data area is needed, <strong>Code end</strong> and <strong>(Program) end</strong> can be set to the same value.
</p>


<h2>COMX and Elf settings</h2>

<p>
The COMX Slot code must be defined as the COMX out 1 definition:
</p>
<ul>
<li>b0: 0=Select ROM bank, 1=Select RAM bank</li>
<li>b1: Select slot 1</li>
<li>b2: Select slot 2</li>
<li>b3: Select slot 3</li>
<li>b4: Select slot 4</li>
<li>b5-b7: bank number 0 to 8</li>
</ul>

<p>
Bits 0 and 5 to 7 are only used in combination with the SB FW.
</p>

<p>
The Elf EMS Page is the page number 0 to 1F.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Note:</strong> Any configuration range in a COMX Slot must always be between hex C000 and hex DFFF and the Elf Page between hex 8000 and hex BFFF.
</p>


<h2>Multiple Programs in one configuration</h2>

<p>
The multiple program configuration interface is shown below:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_conf2.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Program configuration</strong> number</li>
<li>(2) <strong>New</strong> button</li>
<li>(3) <strong>Store</strong> button</li>
<li>(4) <strong>Configuration</strong> spin button</li>
<li>(5) <strong>Save on exit</strong> checkbox</li>
<li>(6) <strong>SB</strong> button</li>
</ul>

<p>
To add a program to a configuration, press the <strong>New</strong> button (2).<br>
<strong>Result:</strong> the <strong>Store</strong> button (3) turns red.
</p>

<p>
Fill in the <strong>Program name</strong>, <strong>Code start</strong>, <strong>Code end</strong>, and <strong>(Program) end</strong> fields, then press <strong>Store</strong> (3).
</p>

<p>
Press the <strong>Configuration</strong> spin button (4) to step through the defined configurations (only possible if more than one is defined). The current program configuration number is shown in field (1).
</p>

<p>
To automatically save the defined programs as well as the <tt>.config</tt> file, enable the <strong>Save on exit</strong> checkbox (5). If no configuration file has been specified, a default file <tt>debug.config</tt> will be saved.
</p>

<p>
All areas defined as code will show the address locations in black; other areas will show the address in grey.
</p>

<p>
Multiple program areas can be defined in one configuration. For example, loading the SB configuration (press the <strong>SB</strong> button (6) on a COMX with SB active) will load 15 program areas.
</p>


<h2>Configuration version</h2>

<p>
After every program configuration <strong>Save</strong>, all binary and debug files are backed up by appending a version number extension to the file name. Version numbers start at 1 and count up.
</p>

<p>
The configuration version interface is shown below:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_version.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Configuration version</strong> text field</li>
</ul>

<p>
The <strong>Direct Assembler</strong> can load any of these configuration versions back by filling in a number in the <strong>Configuration version</strong> text field (1). To load back the oldest backup configuration, fill in <tt>1</tt>; to load back the latest, fill in the latest number found in the directory where all configurations are saved. The example above would load back version 4.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Note:</strong> All files that have the same configuration extension will be loaded. After adding an additional program into a configuration, that program will start with version 1 while older programs may already have a higher version number. Therefore, delete or move older versions when adding a new program to a configuration.
</p>
</body>
</html>
